"Instruction-Following Evaluation for Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2311.07911v1)\nciteturn56view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn49view4\",\"lineno\":77}); Total lines: 2099\nL73: Change Cases  | All Lowercase  | Your entire response should be in English, and in all lowercase letters. No capital letters are allowed.\nL74: Change Cases  | Frequency of All-capital Words  | In your response, words with all capital letters should appear at least / around / at most {N} times.\nL75: Start with / End with  | End Checker  | Finish your response with this exact phrase {end_phrase}. No other words should follow this phrase.\nL76: Start with / End with  | Quotation  | Wrap your entire response with double quotation marks.\nL77: Punctuation  | No Commas  | In your entire response, refrain from the use of any commas.\nL78: Table 1: The list of 25 verifiable instructions, with brief descriptions. We use these instructions because we think they are either easy to verify or common in real-world applications. The list can be expanded trivially. For example, one can add “Language - Mixed Two Languages in Response” and “Detectable Format - XML Format”.\nL79: In summary, we propose IFEval: Instruction-Following Eval, a benchmark to evaluate the instruction following ability of LLMs using a set of prompts containing verifiable instructions. These verifiable instructions are atomic instructions for which one can use a simple, interpretable, and deterministic program to verify if corresponding responses follow the instructions or not.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFAQ - Ollama (https://docs.ollama.com/faq)\nciteturn56view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.ollama.com/faq\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 473\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStreaming - Ollama (https://docs.ollama.com/api/streaming)\nciteturn56view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.ollama.com/api/streaming\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 60\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRouteLLM: Learning to Route LLMs with Preference Data (https://arxiv.org/html/2406.18665v4)\nciteturn56view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2406.18665v4\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 406\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/file/724be4472168f31ba1c9ac630f15dec8-Paper-Conference.pdf)\nciteturn56view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 27; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn54view0\",\"pattern\":\"RadixAttention\"}); Total lines: 1640\nL204@P3: (e.g., dynamic tree structures). Consequently, most state-of-the-art inference systems recompute the\nL205@P3: KV cache for each request. We will discuss their limitations and our differences in Sec. 7.\nL206@P3: This section introduces RadixAttention, a novel technique for automatic and systematic KV cache\nL207@P3: reuse during runtime. Unlike existing systems that discard the KV cache after a generation request\nL208@P3: finishes, our system retains the cache for prompts and generation results in a radix tree, enabling\nL209@P3: efficient prefix search, reuse, insertion, and eviction. We implement an LRU eviction policy and\nL210@P3: a cache-aware scheduling policy to enhance the cache hit rate. RadixAttention is compatible with\nL211@P3: techniques like continuous batching [60], paged attention [23], and tensor parallelism [44]. In\nL212@P3: addition, it introduces only negligible memory and time overhead when there is no cache hit.\nL213@P3: RadixAttention. A radix tree is a data structure that serves as a space-efficient alternative to a\nL542@P7: Figure 7: Normalized throughput on Mixtral-8x7B models with tensor parallelism. Higher is better.\nL543@P7: result from KV cache reuse, the exploitation of parallelism within a single program, and faster\nL544@P7: constrained decoding. Next, we explain the reasons for the speedup in each benchmark.\nL545@P7: On MMLU, SGLang can reuse the KV cache of the 5-shot examples with RadixAttention. RadixAt\u0002tention benefits both throughput and latency. RadixAttention reduces total memory usage by sharing\nL546@P7: the KV cache, allowing for a larger batch size to improve maximum throughput. RadixAttention\nL547@P7: also reduces the computation of prefill, thus decreasing the first token latency. On HellaSwag,\nL548@P7: SGLang reuses the KV cache of both few-shot examples and the common question prefix for multiple\nL549@P7: choices, resulting in two-level sharing. For the ReAct and generative agents, SGLang reuses the\nL550@P7: KV cache of the agent template and previous calls. On Tree-of-thought and Skeleton-of-thought,\nL565@P7: indicating that our optimization generalizes well to larger models. We omit Guidance and LMQL\nL566@P7: here because they lack efficient implementations of tensor parallelism.\nL567@P7: Results on multi-modal models. SGLang has native support for multi-modal models with the\nL568@P7: image and video primitives. The optimizations in this paper are compatible with multi-modal\nL569@P7: models. For RadixAttention, we compute the hash of the input images and use it as the key in the\nL575@P7: multiple questions about the same image, and SGLang runtime reuses the KV cache in this case.\nL576@P7: Production deployment. SGLang has been deployed in Chatbot Arena [8] to serve open-weight\nL577@P7: models. Due to low traffic for some models, only one SGLang worker serves each. After one\nL578@P7: month, we observed a 52.4% RadixAttention cache hit rate for LLaVA-Next-34B [28] and 74.1% for\nL579@P7: Vicuna-33B [7]. Cache hits come from common system messages, frequently reused example images,\nL600@P8: First Token Latency (s)\nL601@P8: LLM Judge Tree of\nL602@P8: Thought\nL603@P8: MMLU Multi-Turn\nL604@P8: Chat(short)\nL605@P8: 0.00\nL606@P8: 0.25\nL607@P8: 0.50\nL608@P8: 0.75\nL609@P8: 1.00\nL610@P8: Throughput\nL611@P8: (Normalized)\nL612@P8: (c)\nL613@P8: No Cache\nL614@P8: No Tree Structure\nL615@P8: FCFS Schedule\nL616@P8: Random Schedule\nL617@P8: No Frontend Parallelism\nL618@P8: No Frontend Hint\nL619@P8: Full Optimization\nL620@P8: 0.4k\nL621@P8: 0.6k\nL622@P8: 0.8k\nL623@P8: 1.0k\nL624@P8: 1.2k\nL625@P8: 10\nL626@P8: 20\nL627@P8: Figure 8: (a)(b) Cache hit rate ablation study. (c) RadixAttention ablation study.\nL628@P8: Results on API models. We test a prompt that extracts three fields from a Wikipedia page using\nL629@P8: OpenAI’s GPT-3.5 model. By using few-shot prompting, the accuracy of API speculative execution\nL630@P8: is high, and it reduces the cost of input tokens by about threefold due to the extraction of three fields.\nL631@P8: 6.3 Ablation Study\nL632@P8: Cache hit rate vs. latency/throughput. Fig. 8(a)(b) shows the relationship between cache hit\nL633@P8: rate and performance metrics (first token latency, total latency, batch size, and throughput) on the\nL634@P8: tree-of-thought benchmark. The figure is obtained by partially disabling matched tokens at runtime.\nL635@P8: It shows that a higher cache hit rate leads to a larger batch size, higher throughput, and lower latency.\nL636@P8: Effectiveness of RadixAttention. We test the effectiveness of RadixAttention and its components\nL637@P8: on several representative benchmarks. As shown in Fig. 8(c), \"No Cache\" means not using any\nL638@P8: cache, \"No Tree-Structure\" means using a simple table-based cache instead of a tree-structured cache,\nL639@P8: \"FCFS Schedule\" means using a first-come-first-serve policy instead of our cache-aware scheduling,\nL640@P8: \"Random Schedule\" means using a random order to schedule requests, \"No Frontend Parallelism\"\nL641@P8: means disabling parallelism in the interpreter, \"No Frontend Hint\" means disabling sending the\nL642@P8: fork hints from the interpreters, and \"Full optimizations\" means we turn on all optimizations. The\nL643@P8: experimental results show that each of these components is required to achieve the best performance.\nL644@P8: Disabling parallelism and hints from the frontend interpreter also results in suboptimal runtime\nL645@P8: performance, highlighting the importance of co-designing the frontend language and runtime.\nL646@P8: Overhead of RadixAttention. We test the overhead of RadixAttention on a benchmark without any\nL647@P8: KV cache reuse opportunities. The benchmark measures throughput on the ShareGPT dataset. It\nL648@P8: takes 74.3 seconds to run 100 requests; however, the time used for managing the RadixAttention data\nL649@P8: structures is only 0.2 seconds, which is a negligible overhead of less than 0.3%. This is because the\nL650@P8: complexity of tree operations is linear and small. Thus, we can turn on RadixAttention by default.\nL651@P8: Effectiveness of the compressed finite state machine. We test the effectiveness of the compressed\nL652@P8: finite state machine and its components on the JSON decoding benchmark. Experimental results\nL653@P8: show that the compressed finite state machine increases the throughput by 1.6× because it can decode\nL654@P8: multiple tokens at once. In addition, we need to preprocess the state machine and reuse it for a batch\nL655@P8: of requests. Otherwise, redoing the preprocessing for each request makes the throughput 2.4× lower.\nL656@P8: 7 Related Work\nL657@P8: Various works have explored the reuse of the KV cache, and many of them are concurrent with our\nL658@P8: work. Uniquely, our RadixAttention first proposes treating the KV cache as a tree-based LRU cache.\nL659@P8: It is the first solution that supports multi-level sharing, cache-aware scheduling, frontend-runtime\nL660@P8: co-scheduling, and distributed cases. vLLM [23] and ChunkedAttention [58] explore some simple\nL661@P8: reuse cases (e.g., system prompt sharing) but do not cover multi-level tree-structured sharing or LRU\nL662@P8: caching. PromptCache [12] proposes the modular reuse of the KV cache beyond the prefix but can\nL663@P8: impact accuracy by up to a 43% drop. HydraGen [18], FlashInfer [59], and ChunkedAttention [58]\nL664@P8: focus on CUDA kernel optimizations and do not include the concept of an LRU cache. InferCept [1]\nL665@P8: and LLM-SQL [29] study KV cache reuse for specific applications such as interleaving with external\nL666@P8: API calls and relational databases, but they do not have our radix tree or cache-aware scheduling.\nL667@P8-9: 9Several LLM programming and agent frameworks exist, such as Guidance [13], LMQL [4],\nL668@P9: DSPy [20], LangChain [24], AutoGen [55], and LLM Compiler [21]. Guidance and LMQL are most\nL669@P9: similar to SGLang, and we compare them in Sec. 2. Our innovation lies in novel runtime optimizations\nL670@P9: for accelerating the proposed programming model. SGLang is compatible with other frameworks and\nL671@P9: can accelerate them (e.g., the DSPy example in our evaluation). Additionally, SGLang is compatible\nL672@P9: with many other common inference optimizations [60, 39, 3, 23, 59, 10, 26, 15, 19, 32, 31, 11].\nL673@P9: 8 Future Directions and Conclusion\nL674@P9: Future directions. Despite the progress made with SGLang, several limitations remain that reveal\nL675@P9: promising directions for future research. These include extending SGLang to support additional\nL676@P9: output modalities, adapting RadixAttention to operate across multiple levels of the memory hierarchy\nL677@P9: (e.g., DRAM, Disk) [43], enabling fuzzy semantic matching within RadixAttention, providing higher\u0002level primitives atop SGLang, fixing starvation in cache-aware scheduling [42], and enhancing the\nL678@P9: SGLang compiler to perform advanced static optimizations such as scheduling and memory planning.\nL679@P9: Conclusion. We introduce SGLang, a framework for efficient programming and executing structured\nL680@P9: language model programs. SGLang significantly improves the throughput and latency of complex\nL681@P9: LM programs through novel optimizations like RadixAttention, compressed finite state machines,\nL682@P9: and a language interpreter. It is a valuable tool for developing advanced prompting techniques and\nL683@P9: agent workflows. The source code is publicly available.\nL684@P9: Acknowledgement\nL685@P9: This project is supported by the Stanford Center for Automated Reasoning and gifts from Astronomer,\nL686@P9: Google, IBM, Intel, Lacework, Microsoft, Mohamed Bin Zayed University of Artificial Intelligence,\nL687@P9: Nexla, Samsung SDS, Uber, and VMware. Lianmin Zheng is supported by a Meta Ph.D. Fellowship.\nL688@P9: We thank Yuanhan Zhang and Bo Li for the LLaVA-NeXT (video) support.\nL689@P9: References\nL918@P14: state [25]. Basic prefix sharing has been investigated in vLLM [23]. More advanced sharing patterns\nL919@P14: like irregular tree-structured sharing can also be employed. Fig. 9 shows four typical patterns of KV\nL920@P14: cache sharing across multiple calls; none of the existing systems can automatically handle all of them.\nL921@P14: On the contrary, our RadixAttention in Sec. 3 can handle all of them automatically at runtime.\nL923@P14: Alg. 1 shows the pseudocode of cache-aware scheduling for RadixAttention with continuous batching.\nL924@P14: A.3 Proof of the Theorem 3.1\nL925@P14: Theorem 3.1. For a batch of requests, we can achieve an optimal cache hit rate by visiting the radix\nL926@P14: tree of the requests in the depth-first search order, with a cache size ≥ the maximum request length.\nL927@P14: The longest-shared-prefix-first order is equivalent to a depth-first search order.\nL1032@P16: contains only one leaf node in C\nL1033@P16: (k)\nL1034@P16: that has its\nL1035@P16: subtree in T\nL1036@P16: ′ not fully visited. At this point, we have reached a valid DFS state. The remaining part\nL1037@P16: of T\nL1038@P16: ′ will be visited in DFS order as described in the proof of Theorem 3.1.\nL1039@P16: A.4 Data-Parallel Distributed RadixAttention\nL1040@P16: To adapt the RadixAttention for distributed settings with multiple replica workers (i.e., data par\u0002allelism), we developed a mechanism wherein each worker maintains its own sub-tree, while the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLLMLingua: Compressing Prompts for Accelerated Inference of Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2310.05736v1)\nciteturn56view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn53view8\",\"pattern\":\"budget controller\"}); Total lines: 499\nL15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Work L18:     1. cite8†2.1 Efficient LLMs L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Out-of-Distribution (OoD) Detection L20:     3. cite10†2.3 LLMs as a Compressor L21:   4. cite11†3 Problem Formulation L22:   5. cite12†4 Methodology L23:     1. cite13†4.1 Budget Controller L24:       1. cite14†Derive compression ratio for demonstrations. L25:       2. cite15†Demonstration-level prompt compression. L26:       3. cite16†Adjust compression ratios for instruction and question. L68: To accelerate model inference and reduce cost, this paper presents LLMLingua, a coarse-to-fine prompt compression method that involves a budget controller to maintain semantic integrity under high compression ratios, a token-level iterative compression algorithm to better model the interdependence between compressed contents, and an instruction tuning based method for distribution alignment between language models.\nL78: This paper proposes LLMLingua, a coarse-to-fine prompt compression method, to address the aforementioned issues. Specifically, we first present a budget controller to dynamically allocate different compression ratios to various components in original prompts such as the instruction, demonstrations, and the question, and meanwhile, perform coarse-grained, demonstration-level compression to maintain semantic integrity under high compression ratios.\nL107: In this section, we elaborate on the proposed coarse-to-fine prompt compression approach, LLMLingua. First, we introduce a budget controller to dynamically allocate different compression ratios to various components in prompts and meanwhile, perform coarse-grained, demonstration-level compression to maintain semantic integrity under high compression ratios. Next, we describe the proposed iterative prompt algorithm designed to retain knowledge from the prompt while compressing.\nL108: Finally, we introduce alignment to address the distribution gap between the small model and black-box large models. Figure  cite86†1 show the framework.\nL109: ### 4.1 Budget Controller\nL110: \nL111: The budget controller here is designed to allocate different budgets, i.e., compression ratio, to different components in a prompt such as instructions, demonstrations, and questions, at the sentence or demonstration level. There are two considerations:\nL112: (i) In general, the instruction and the question in a prompt have a direct influence on the generated results, as they should contain all the necessary knowledge to generate the following answer. On the contrary, if there are multiple demonstrations in the original prompt, the conveyed information may be redundant. Therefore, a tailored budget controller is required to allocate more budget (i.e., smaller compression ratios) for instructions and questions, and less budget for demonstrations.\nL113: (ii) When a high compression ratio is required, token-level dropout as in cite58†Li (2023) might make the compressed prompts too trivial and thus lose vital information from the original prompt. Consequently, sentence-level dropout should be employed instead to preserve a certain degree of linguistic integrity. Especially in the case of multiple redundant demonstrations, we can even perform demonstration-level control to meet the compression requirement.\nL114: Algorithm cite87†1 illustrates the overall procedure of the budget controller.\nL115: \nL116: Algorithm 1 Pseudo code of Budget Controller.\nL117: \nL118: Input: A small language model $\\mathcal{M}_{s}$; the original prompt $\\bm{x}=(\\bm{x}^{\\text{ins}},\\bm{x}^{\\text{dems}},\\bm{x}^{\\text{que}})$.\nL119: \nL120: 1: Set the selected demonstration set $\\mathcal{D}=\\phi$.\nL121: \nL122: 2: Get demonstration compression rate $\\tau_{\\text{dem}}$ by Eq.(cite88†2 ).\nL123: \nL124: 3: Calculate the perplexity of each demonstration via $\\mathcal{M}_{s}$.\nL125: 4: Rank all demonstrations in descending order of their perplexity as a list $(\\bm{x}^{\\text{dem}}_{(1)},...,\\bm{x}^{\\text{dem}}_{(N)})$, where $N$ is the number of demonstrations, $\\bm{x}^{\\text{dem}}_{(i)}$ is the $i$-th demonstration.\nL126: \nL127: 5: for $i=1$ do\nL128: \nL129: 6:   if $\\widetilde{L}_{\\mathcal{D}}>k\\cdot\\tau_{\\text{dems}}L_{\\text{dems}}$ then\nL130: \nL131: 7:    Break.\nL132: \nL133: 8:   end if\nL134: \nL135: 9:   Append $\\bm{x}_{(i)}^{\\text{dem}}$ to $\\mathcal{D}$.\nL136: \nL137: 10:   $i=i+1$\nL138: \nL139: 11: end for\nL140: 12: Allocate remaining budget to $\\bm{x}^{\\text{ins}}$ and $\\bm{x}^{\\text{que}}$ via Eq. (cite89†3 ).\nL141: \nL142: Output: The subset of demonstrations $\\mathcal{D}$ obtained from coarse-grained compression; Additional budget $\\Delta\\tau_{\\text{ins},\\text{que}}$ for the instruction and the question.\nL143: #### Derive compression ratio for demonstrations.\nL144: \nL145: We first compute the compression rate for demonstrations $\\tau_{\\text{dems}}$ according to the target overall compression rate $\\tau$ and the pre-defined compression rate for instructions and questions, i.e., $\\tau_{\\text{ins}}$ and $\\tau_{\\text{que}}$, respectively.\nL146: \nL147:  | $$\\tau_{\\text{dems}}=\\frac{\\tau L-(\\tau_{\\text{ins}}L_{\\text{ins}}+\\tau_{\\text{que}}L_{\\text{que}})}{L_{\\text{dems}}}.$$  |  | (2)\nL159: ### 4.2 Iterative Token-level Prompt Compression\nL160: \nL161: Algorithm 2 Pseudo code of Iterative Token-level Prompt Compression (ITPC).\nL162: \nL163: Input: A small language model $\\mathcal{M}_{s}$; the prompt from budget controller $\\bm{x}^{\\prime}=(\\bm{x}^{\\text{ins}},\\bm{x}^{\\mathcal{D}},\\bm{x}^{\\text{que}})$; target compression rate$\\tau$, adjusted compression rate $\\triangle\\tau_{\\text{ins},\\text{que}}$.\nL164: \nL165: 1: Set the selected token set $\\mathcal{T}=\\phi$\nL166: \nL167: 2: Get segment set $\\mathcal{S}$.\nL168: \nL169: 3: for $i=1,2,\\ldots,m$ do\nL281: To validate the contributions of different components in our approach, we introduce five variants of our model for ablation study: i) Ours w/o Iterative Token-level Compression, which performs token-level compression in a single inference rather than iteratively. ii) Ours w/o Budget Controller, which directly employs ITPC with the same compression ratio for all components. iii) Ours w/o Dynamic Compression Ratio, which uses the same compression ratio for all components.\nL282: iv) Ours w/ Random Selection in Budget Controller, which randomly selects demonstrations or sentences for demonstration-level prompt compression. v) Ours w/o Distribution Alignment, which removes the distribution alignment module of our approach and directly use the pre-trained LLaMA-7B as the small language model. vi) Ours w/ Remove Stop Words, which removes the stop words in original prompts using NLTK^{7}^{7} 7 https://www.nltk.org/. Table cite104†3 shows the results.\nL283:  | EM  | Tokens  | $1/\\tau$\nL284: Ours  | 79.08  | 439  | 5x\nL285: - w/o Iterative Token-level Prompt Compression  | 72.93  | 453  | 5x\nL286: - w/o Budget Controller  | 73.62  | 486  | 5x\nL287: - w/o Dynamic Compression Ratio  | 77.26  | 457  | 5x\nL288: - w/ Random Selection in Budget Controller  | 72.78  | 477  | 5x\nL289: - w/o Distribution Alignment  | 78.62  | 452  | 5x\nL290: - w/ Remove Stop Words  | 76.27  | 1,882  | 1.3x\nL291: \nL292: Table 3: Ablation study on GSM8K in 1-shot constraint.\nL293: Comparing Ours with w/o Iterative Token-level Prompt Compression, we observe a significant decline in Exact Match when the conditional dependence between compressed tokens is not considered. We conjecture this variant may lose essential information in the prompt, especially for low-frequency keywords that frequently appear in the given prompt.\nL294: When comparing Ours with w/o Dynamic Compression Ratio and with w/o Budget Controller, it reveals that different components of the prompt exhibit varying sensitivity. Instructions and questions necessitate a lower compression ratio. To balance the relationship between compression ratio and language integrity, introducing a demonstration or sentence-level filter better preserves sufficient linguistic information, even at higher compression ratios.\nL295: Ours w/ Random Selection in Budget Controller indicates that selecting sentences or demonstrations based on perplexity can better identify information-rich sentences for target LLMs. Distribution Alignment allows small LMs to generate distributions that more closely resemble those of target LLMs, resulting in a further improvement of 0.56 on GSM8K.\nL296: ### 5.5 Discussion\nL297: #### Different Target LLMs\nL298: Here we test our method with Claude-v1.3 as the target LLM to demonstrate its generalizability across different black-box LLMs in addition to the GPT series models. Due to the limitation of API cost, we only consider the scenarios with one-shot constraint and half-shot constraint. Similarly, we employe Alpaca-7B as the small language model for the challenges in collecting alignment data.\nL299: As shown in Table cite105†4 , our method can achieve improvements over the simple prompt by 0.8 and 1.7 EM points with compression ratios of 5x and 14x, respectively.\nL300:  | EM  | Tokens  | $1/\\tau$\nL301: Ours in 1-shot constraint  | 83.51  | 439  | 5x\nL302: Ours in half-shot constraint  | 82.61  | 171  | 14x\nL303: Simple Prompt  | 81.8  | 691  | 3x\nL304: \nL305: Table 4: Ours method on GSM8K using Claude-v1.3.\nL306: #### Different Small LMs\nL307: We further test our approach with different small language models: we fine-tune the GPT2-small on the Alpaca dataset and use it as the small LM for our system. As shown in Table cite106†5 , the results obtained by Alpaca finetuned GPT2-small are weaker than those obtained by Alpaca-7B with a performance drop of 2.06, 0.99, and 1.06 EM points at different compression ratios. This is due to the significant distribution discrepancy between the small LM and the target LLM.\nL308: Even with distribution alignment, it is still difficult to directly estimate the target LLM using the distribution from the small language model. Similar observations have been reported in cite58†Li (2023) . However, benefiting from the proposed budget controller and the iterative token-level prompt compression algorithm, our approach achieves satisfactory results in difficult tasks such as reasoning even with the less powerful GPT2-Small as the small language model.\nL309:  | EM  | Tokens  | $1/\\tau$\nL310: Ours with GPT2 in 1-shot constraint  | 77.02  | 447  | 5x\nL311: Ours with GPT2 in half-shot constraint  | 76.42  | 173  | 14x\nL312: Ours with GPT2 in quarter-shot constraint  | 76.27  | 128  | 18x\nL313: \nL314: Table 5: Our method on GSM8K with GPT2-Alpaca as the small language model.\nL315: #### The Generation Results of Compressed Prompt\nL316: \nL317: Appendix cite48†E displays several compressed prompts along with following generation texts. It is evident that the compressed prompts can still guide the generation of multi-step reasoning outcomes similar to the original ones. In contrast, prompts compressed using Selective-Context exhibit errors in reasoning logic. This highlights the effectiveness of our method in preserving crucial semantic information while retaining reasoning capabilities.\nL318: Figure 2: The distribution of generated token lengths at varying compression ratios ($1/\\tau$).\nL319: As depicted in Figure cite107†2 , we also analyze the relationship between the compression ratio and the length of the corresponding generated texts. It can be observed that as the compression ratio increases, the text length produced by target LLMs tends to decrease, albeit with varying degrees across different datasets. This indicates that prompt compression not only saves computational resources in the input but also contributes to computational savings in the generation stage.\nL320: #### Overhead of LLMLingua\nL321: \nL322: We explore two key factors to study the computation overhead of LLMLingua: the number of tokens involved in computation and the end-to-end latency.\nL323: \nL324: The overall computation of our system is the sum of the prompt compression and the following inference. This can be formulated as:\nL325: \nL326:  | $$c=(L+kL/\\tau+L/\\tau)\\cdot c_{\\text{small}}+L/\\tau\\cdot c_{\\text{LLMs}},$$  |  | (9)\nL327: where $c_{\\text{small}}$ and $c_{\\text{LLMs}}$ represent the per token computation load of the small LM and LLM, respectively. $L$, $kL/\\tau$, and $L/\\tau$ are the numbers of token inferences for the budget controller, the perplexity calculation of tokens to compress in ITPC, and the conditioned perplexity calculation of compressed results in ITPC (using KV cache), respectively.\nL347: Our method is orthogonal to Prompt Engineering methods, such as prompt retrieval and prompt ordering. Our work focuses on compressing well-designed prompts, and it performs well on complex and fine-tuned prompts like GSM8K. Moreover, the perplexity-based demonstration filtering method used in our budget controller can also be applied to scenarios such as prompt retrieval. This demonstrates the compatibility and adaptability of our approach in various LLMs settings.\nL348: ## 6 Conclusion\nL349: We introduce a coarse-to-fine algorithm for prompt compression, named LLMLingua, which is based on the small LM’s PPL for black-box LLMs. Our approach consists of three modules: Budget Controller, Iterative Token-level Compression, and Alignment.\nL350: We validate the effectiveness of our approach on 4 datasets from different domains, i.e., GSM8K, BBH, ShareGPT, and Arxiv-March23, demonstrating that our method achieves state-of-the-art performance across all datasets, with up to 20x compression with only a 1.5 point performance drop. Moreover, we observe that LLMs can effectively restore compressed prompts, and prompt compression contributes to a reduction in generated text length.\nL351: Our approach holds substantial practical implications, as it not only reduces computational costs but also offers a potential solution for accommodating longer contexts in LLMs. The method of compressing prompts has the potential to enhance downstream task performance by compressing longer prompts and to improve the LLMs’s inference efficiency by compressing the KV cache.\nL352: ## Limitations\nL353: \nL354: There are also some limitations in our approach. For instance, we might observe a notable performance drop when trying to achieve excessively high compression ratios such as 25x-30x on GSM8K, as shown in Figure cite111†3 .\nL355: \nL356: Figure 3: The performance of various prompt compression methods at different compression ratios ($1/\\tau$) on GSM8K. The dashed line corresponds to the Exact Match score obtained from the full-shot prompt.\nL357: It is shown that as the compression ratio increases especially around 25x-30x, all methods as well as ours will experience a substantial performance drop. In comparison with other methods, this performance drop derived from our approach is significantly shifted to much higher compression ratios. We owe this to the Budget Controller and the Iterative Token-level Prompt Compression algorithm, which enable our method to maintain the original prompt information even at some extreme compression ratios.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCorrective Retrieval Augmented Generation (https://arxiv.org/html/2401.15884v3)\nciteturn56view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn53view6\",\"pattern\":\"Ambiguous\"}); Total lines: 453\nL153: Besides, a retrieval is assumed Incorrect when the confidence scores of all retrieved documents are below the lower threshold. This indicates that all retrieved documents are considered irrelevant, which are unhelpful for generation. Once the knowledge from the retrieval results is judged to be inaccurate, it is unwise to still get stuck in it, which is likely to result in fabricated facts. Therefore, we need to seek new sources of knowledge for correction.\nL154: Here, web search is introduced to search from the Internet as elaborated in Section cite21†4.5 . This corrective action helps overcome the embarrassing challenge where no reliable knowledge can be referred to.\nL155: #### Ambiguous\nL156: Except for the above two situations, the remaining will be assigned to an intermediate action of Ambiguous. This generally occurs when the accuracy of the retrieval is hard to distinguish and the evaluator gives an intermediate score. Since the retrieval evaluator is not confident in its judgment, both types of processed knowledge in Correct and Incorrect are combined to complement each other.\nL157: Implementing such a moderating and soft strategy can significantly contribute to strengthening the robustness and resilience of the system, fostering a more adaptable framework for optimal performance.\nL158: #### Discussion\nL159: \nL160: Preliminary experiments of employing only the Correct and Incorrect actions show that the efficacy of CRAG was easily affected by the accuracy of the retrieval evaluator. The reason might be the distinct knowledge switch for all input cases, regardless of the level of confidence in their judgment. The design of the Ambiguous action significantly helps to mitigate the dependence on the accuracy of the retrieval evaluator.\n"
